"""Original muscles and nervous system drill; the first option is correct."""
RAW = '''
Muscle attached to the skeleton through tendons is|skeletal muscle|cardiac muscle|smooth muscle|muscle found only in the heart|Skeletal muscle acts on bones through tendons. Tendons transmit force; ligaments instead join bones.
The muscle forming the heart wall is|cardiac muscle|skeletal muscle|smooth muscle|voluntary skeletal muscle alone|Cardiac muscle forms the muscular wall of the heart. Its contractions pump blood and are not usually under deliberate control.
Muscle in the walls of structures such as the digestive tract is|smooth muscle|skeletal muscle|cardiac muscle|skeletal muscle only in limbs|Smooth muscle is found in walls of structures including the digestive tract. Its activity is generally involuntary and differs from skeletal muscle.
Skeletal muscle is generally under|voluntary control, with reflex and automatic contributions possible|exclusively unconscious control with no voluntary action|no nervous control at all|only direct conscious control without reflex involvement|Skeletal muscle is generally voluntary but can also take part in reflexes and automatic adjustments. A reflex can activate skeletal muscle without a deliberate choice.
Cardiac muscle activity is generally|involuntary|controlled entirely by deliberate choice|identical to voluntary elbow movement|normally controlled by deliberate instructions for every beat|Cardiac muscle activity is generally involuntary. The heartbeat is not normally initiated by conscious commands.
Smooth muscle activity is generally|involuntary|normally controlled only by deliberate choice|the same as conscious elbow flexion|always initiated by a voluntary instruction|Smooth muscle activity is generally involuntary. The movement of food through the gut illustrates involuntary muscular activity.
A tendon connects|muscle to bone|bone to bone|muscle to muscle at every joint|bone to cartilage at every joint|A tendon connects muscle to bone. Ligaments instead usually connect one bone to another.
Skeletal muscle commonly moves a bone by|pulling through a tendon|pushing in every direction without contracting|moving the bone without attachment|lengthening passively as the sole source of force|A skeletal muscle pulls on a bone through a tendon. A muscle generates a pulling force rather than directly pushing a bone.
An antagonistic pair is a simplified way to describe muscles that|contribute to movement in opposite directions|produce exactly the same action at every joint|never activate at the same time|are both types of joint|Antagonistic muscles contribute to opposing actions. The pair is a model of opposing actions, not a claim that no other muscles work.
The biceps brachii contributes to|elbow flexion|elbow extension|knee extension|ankle plantar flexion|The biceps contributes to bending the elbow. Elbow flexion generally decreases the angle between the arm and forearm.
The triceps brachii contributes to|elbow extension|elbow flexion|knee flexion|ankle dorsiflexion|The triceps contributes to straightening the elbow. Elbow extension generally increases the angle at the joint.
Bending the elbow is commonly called|flexion|extension|abduction|rotation|Elbow bending is flexion. Extension is the opposite movement in this simple comparison.
Straightening the elbow is commonly called|extension|flexion|adduction|rotation|Elbow straightening is extension. Flexion is the opposite movement in this simple comparison.
The biceps and triceps example is simplified because|other muscles and joint-control mechanisms also contribute|only the biceps is present in the arm|the elbow has no other supporting structures|muscles cannot co-activate|Other muscles and co-activation can contribute to elbow movement and stability. Co-activation can support stability even when one action is the main focus.
Muscles on opposite sides of a joint can sometimes|be active together to help control and stabilise movement|never be active at the same time|work without using energy|only act when all other muscles are inactive|Co-activation can help control and stabilise a joint. Stabilising a joint does not mean that opposing muscles must never contract together.
An isometric action involves|force without appreciable change in overall muscle length|shortening while producing force in every case|lengthening while producing force in every case|complete absence of muscle force|Isometric action produces force with little or no overall change in muscle length.
Holding a position while producing muscle force can be|an isometric action|a concentric action in every case|an eccentric action in every case|proof that no muscle is active|Holding a position can involve isometric muscle action.
Muscle contraction does not always mean|the muscle becomes shorter|the muscle can generate force|the nervous system may be involved|other muscles may contribute to control|Isometric action demonstrates force without appreciable shortening.
The central nervous system consists of the|brain and spinal cord|peripheral nerves alone|brain and peripheral nerves without the spinal cord|spinal cord and peripheral nerves without the brain|The brain and spinal cord form the central nervous system.
Nerves connect the central nervous system with|other parts of the body|the heart alone|the limbs alone|the skull alone|Nerves carry signals between the central nervous system and other tissues.
Sensory information can|inform a response to a stimulus|guarantee a safe response in all circumstances|replace the need for motor pathways|eliminate the influence of the environment|Sensory information can help the nervous system respond.
Motor pathways contribute to|signals that activate muscles|signals from receptors towards the central nervous system only|signals that have no effect on effectors|only the storage of sensory information|Motor pathways carry signals involved in muscle activation.
A receptor in a simplified reflex pathway|detects a stimulus|carries the final motor response to a muscle|is always the muscle producing the response|is always the spinal cord|A receptor detects a stimulus.
A muscle responding in a reflex pathway can act as an|effector|receptor|sensory neuron|stimulus|An effector performs the response, such as muscle contraction.
A reflex is|a relatively rapid response involving a neural pathway|always a consciously planned response|a response that never involves nerves|a response requiring a deliberate decision before movement|A reflex involves a neural pathway producing a response.
A simplified reflex arc includes|a receptor, sensory pathway, central connection and motor pathway|a receptor and effector without any neural pathway|a motor pathway without a stimulus|a sensory receptor without any connection to a response|A simple reflex model traces sensory information and a motor response.
A reflex response does not prove that|the brain has no role in processing the event|a neural pathway may be involved|a stimulus can be detected|a muscle may act as an effector|Reflex pathways vary, and the brain may also process the event.
Movement coordination involves|the nervous system, muscles, joints and environment|a single bone acting independently|muscles without input from the nervous system|a fixed pattern unaffected by sensory information|Movement depends on interacting body systems and surroundings.
Fatigue can affect|performance and coordination|only performance without influencing coordination|coordination in exactly the same way in everyone|neither movement nor decision-making|Fatigue can alter performance and coordination.
A warm-up can|help prepare for activity without eliminating every risk|guarantee that injury is impossible|replace all recovery after activity|remove the need to inspect the setting|Preparation can help but does not remove every hazard.
Gradual progression means|adjusting activity demands appropriately over time|raising effort regardless of symptoms|removing every rest period|using identical loads for all learners|Progression should suit the person and context.
A participant with concerning symptoms should|stop and obtain appropriate assistance|continue until the session is complete regardless of severity|diagnose the cause from a diagram|ignore the report if the participant is experienced|Concerning symptoms should be taken seriously and assessed appropriately.
Which muscle type is found in the heart?|cardiac|smooth muscle|skeletal muscle|voluntary skeletal muscle only|Cardiac muscle is found in the heart wall.
Which structure transmits muscle force to bone?|tendon|ligament|articular cartilage|joint capsule alone|A tendon connects muscle to bone and transmits force.
Which structure connects bone to bone?|ligament|tendon|skeletal muscle|articular cartilage|Ligaments connect bones and help stabilise joints.
A motor response is carried towards an effector through a|motor pathway|sensory pathway|receptor alone|afferent pathway towards the central nervous system|A motor pathway carries signals to effectors such as muscles.
The brain belongs to the|central nervous system|peripheral nervous system alone|cardiovascular system|appendicular skeleton|The brain is part of the central nervous system.
The spinal cord belongs to the|central nervous system|peripheral nervous system alone|digestive system|cardiovascular system|The spinal cord is part of the central nervous system.
A muscle producing force during a held position demonstrates that|force can occur without appreciable overall shortening|muscle force requires visible shortening|a held posture never requires muscle activity|isometric activity involves no force|Isometric action can produce force without appreciable change in muscle length.
Which description of the triceps is appropriate?|it contributes to elbow extension|it contributes to elbow flexion as its principal action|it is the prime mover of knee extension|it is the primary muscle of quiet inhalation|The triceps contributes to elbow extension.
'''
ITEMS = []
for line in RAW.strip().splitlines():
    stem, correct, wrong1, wrong2, wrong3, explanation = line.split('|')
    ITEMS.append((stem, (correct, wrong1, wrong2, wrong3), explanation))
